body_text:
This code should be ran with a libtorch build that doesn't link to CUDA. It runs `tensor.to()` using a plain `torch::kCUDA` spec with no device index and a spec with an explicit index 0, printing the error messages in the thrown exceptions:
```python
#include <torch/torch.h>
#include <iostream>

int main() {
   torch::Tensor x = torch::tensor({1.0f, 2.0f, 3.0f});

   try {
       auto opts1 = torch::TensorOptions().dtype(torch::kFloat32)
                        .device(torch::Device(torch::kCUDA));
       auto y1 = x.to(opts1, false, true);
   } catch (const c10::Error& e) {
       std::cout << "Case 1 (\"CUDA\", no index):\n" << e.what() << "\n\n";
   }

   try {
       auto opts2 = torch::TensorOptions().dtype(torch::kFloat32)
                        .device(torch::Device(torch::kCUDA, 0));
       auto y2 = x.to(opts2, false, true);
   } catch (const c10::Error& e) {
       std::cout << "Case 2 ({\"CUDA\", 0}):\n" << e.what() << "\n";
   }

   return 0;
} 
```

The "no index" case fails cleanly with "PyTorch is not linked with support for cuda devices", while the explicit index case fails with a very long message starting with "Could not run 'aten::empty_strided' with arguments from the 'CUDA' backend."

I see this on Linux, PyTorch version 2.13.0

cc @jbschlosser @malfet

