body_text:
TorchDynamo guard checks are tied to Python (torch/_dynamo/guards.py). Evaluating large guard trees on hot paths forces Python frame re-entry, causing GIL contention during dynamic shape validation.

Additionally, standalone C++ runtimes (like AOTInductor) cannot evaluate or inspect guard specs natively before calling generated binaries. Parsing megabytes of TORCH_LOGS="guards" text remains the only way to debug failures.

Move guard specification storage into c10::core. This enables zero-overhead C++ guard evaluation, provides AOTInductor parity, and exposes a clean Python/C++ introspection handle.


```
// c10/core/GuardManager.h
namespace c10 {

enum class GuardKind : uint8_t { TensorShape, TensorDtype, SymIntRel, GlobalState };

struct C10_API GuardSpec {
  GuardKind kind;
  std::string target_expr;
  bool (*native_check_fn)(const at::Tensor&);
};

class C10_API GuardEvaluator {
 public:
  static bool evaluate_native_guards(uint64_t code_id, c10::ArrayRef<at::Tensor> inputs);
  static std::vector<GuardSpec> get_failed_guards(uint64_t code_id, c10::ArrayRef<at::Tensor> inputs);
};

} // namespace c10
```

```

import torch

# GIL-released, low-latency guard evaluation
result = torch._C._dynamo.eval_guards_native(compiled_fn, sample_inputs)
# Returns: {"passed": False, "failing_expr": "arg0.shape[0] == 8", "latency_ns": 42}
```

Key Benefits
Fast Path Speed: Replaces Python frame checks with direct c10::ArrayRef<at::Tensor> bitwise/pointer validation.

AOTInductor Readiness: Native binaries can validate input guards in pure C++.

Structured Diagnostics: Inspect failing guards programmatically in CI unit tests without text-scraping stderr logs.

