body_text:
### 🚀 The feature, motivation and pitch

When we are debugging an accuracy issue from FLUX pipeline, we found that there are some layers of the model are prone to numeric drift causing by torch.compile when operations are fused with a higher precision. 

For example, we found that time_text_embed layer of FluxTransformer2DModel is such a layer. Below is the skeleton of the forward function of FluxTransformer2DModel I used for debugging. If we compile the full model running on BFloat16, we found that results of time_text_embed gets very large numeric drift comparing with eager. 

```
def forward(
        self,
        hidden_states: torch.Tensor,
        encoder_hidden_states: torch.Tensor,
        pooled_projections: torch.Tensor,
        timestep: torch.Tensor,
        img_ids: torch.Tensor,
        txt_ids: torch.Tensor,
        guidance: torch.Tensor | None = None,
        joint_attention_kwargs: dict[str, Any] | None = None,
    ) -> tuple[
        torch.Tensor,
        torch.Tensor,
        torch.Tensor,
        torch.Tensor,
        torch.Tensor,
        torch.Tensor,
        torch.Tensor,
    ]:
        t = self.transformer

        hidden_states = t.x_embedder(hidden_states)
        x_embed_out = hidden_states

        timestep = timestep.to(hidden_states.dtype) * 1000
        if guidance is not None:
            guidance = guidance.to(hidden_states.dtype) * 1000

        temb = (
                t.time_text_embed(timestep, pooled_projections)
                if guidance is None
                else t.time_text_embed(timestep, guidance, pooled_projections)
            )
        encoder_hidden_states = t.context_embedder(encoder_hidden_states)

        if txt_ids.ndim == 3:
            txt_ids = txt_ids[0]
        if img_ids.ndim == 3:
            img_ids = img_ids[0]

        ids = torch.cat((txt_ids, img_ids), dim=0)
        image_rotary_emb = t.pos_embed(ids)

        local_kwargs = dict(joint_attention_kwargs or {})
        if "ip_adapter_image_embeds" in local_kwargs:
            ip_adapter_image_embeds = local_kwargs.pop("ip_adapter_image_embeds")
            ip_hidden_states = t.encoder_hid_proj(ip_adapter_image_embeds)
            local_kwargs.update({"ip_hidden_states": ip_hidden_states})

        # Skip transformer_blocks and single_transformer_blocks on purpose.
        hidden_states = t.norm_out(hidden_states, temb)
        norm_out_out = hidden_states
        output = t.proj_out(hidden_states)

        return (
            x_embed_out,
            temb,
            norm_out_out,
            output,
            encoder_hidden_states,
            image_rotary_emb[0],
            image_rotary_emb[1],
        )

```
If we add a graph break before temb= time_text_embed layer, temb numeric drift of torch compile getting back to normal.

We created script to reproduce the behavior:

[repro_time_text_embed_compile_diff.py](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/32182887/repro_time_text_embed_compile_diff.py)

Running the script:
`python3 repro_time_text_embed_compile_diff.py --device cpu --dtypes bfloat16 --batch-size 1 --image-seq-len 8 --in-channels 64 --embedding-dim 64 --pooled-projection-dim 128 --timestep-scale 1000`

It show very big numeric drift on stage_time_text_embed (max_abs=2.822266e-01 mean_abs=7.722092e-02 rmse=9.964155e-02 rel_l2=3.929916e-01): 

```
[RUN] dtype=bfloat16
  [SCENARIO] name=single_run graph_break=False
    stage_x_embedder: close=True exact=True max_abs=0.000000e+00 mean_abs=0.000000e+00 rmse=0.000000e+00 rel_l2=0.000000e+00
    stage_time_text_embed: close=False exact=False max_abs=2.822266e-01 mean_abs=7.722092e-02 rmse=9.964155e-02 rel_l2=3.929916e-01
    stage_norm_out: close=False exact=False max_abs=2.343750e-01 mean_abs=2.697963e-02 rmse=3.687467e-02 rel_l2=3.687517e-02
    stage_proj_out: close=False exact=False max_abs=6.738281e-02 mean_abs=1.565552e-02 rmse=2.008618e-02 rel_l2=3.435689e-02
  [RESULT] PASS=False atol=0.01 rtol=0.01
```

Either graph break before time_text_embed layer or set torch._inductor.config.emulate_precision_casts=True fix the problem. 

`python3 repro_time_text_embed_compile_diff.py --device cpu --dtypes bfloat16 --batch-size 1 --image-seq-len 8 --in-channels 64 --embedding-dim 64 --pooled-projection-dim 128 --timestep-scale 1000 --graph-break-around-time-text-embed`

or 

`python3 repro_time_text_embed_compile_diff.py --device cpu --dtypes bfloat16 --batch-size 1 --image-seq-len 8 --in-channels 64 --embedding-dim 64 --pooled-projection-dim 128 --timestep-scale 1000 --emulate-precision-casts`

The huge numeric drift is caused by operation fusions running on float32:

```
